    summary_en: "According to the latest data, cybercriminals are shifting their targets from healthcare providers like hospitals and clinics to service providers and medical enterprises. In the first half of 2026, overall attack incidents in the healthcare industry grew by 14%, but attacks on medical enterprises surged by 35%, representing a 110% increase compared to the same period last year. By targeting these central hubs, attackers can simultaneously acquire data and services from multiple hospitals. Specific cases include the Mississippi University Medical Center suffering a ransomware attack that led to operational shutdowns exceeding two weeks; and the German medical billing service provider Unimed was also hacked, resulting in the leakage of sensitive health data belonging to tens of thousands of patients. Industry experts point out that healthcare institutions face challenges including patching legacy equipment, resource shortages, and high reliance on third-party services. Experts recommend that in addition to strengthening basic security measures such as remote access and multi-factor authentication, special attention should be paid to the cybersecurity resilience of the supply chain and third-party service providers to mitigate overall risk.",
