    title_en: "Former DigitalMint Employees Charged with Involvement in BlackCat Ransomware Attack, Sentenced to Prison",
    summary_en: "Angelo Martino, Kevin Tyler Martin, and Ryan Clifford Goldberg, former employees of the cybersecurity incident response company DigitalMint, were charged with extortion and obstruction of business for their involvement in the BlackCat (ALPHV) ransomware attack. According to court documents, these employees utilized the BlackCat ransomware to attack multiple US organizations between 2023 and 2025, demanding ransoms from the victims. They were responsible not only for encrypting the victims' servers but also for providing the hacker operators with confidential information regarding the victims' insurance policies and negotiation positions, using their role as negotiation representatives to maximize the ransom amount. Victims included financial service companies, non-profit organizations, school districts, and healthcare institutions. Among these, some victims paid tens of millions of dollars in ransoms. DigitalMint strongly condemned the employees' actions and has already terminated their employment. This case highlights the potential for cybersecurity service provider employees to be exploited for criminal activities, emphasizing the need for internal controls and ethical risk management within cybersecurity service processes.",
