    summary_en: "The U.S. Department of Commerce announced the easing of export controls targeting the UAE, allowing specific U.S.-headquartered companies to more easily transport advanced computing chips, servers, and other controlled technologies to the region. Apple is one of the eight companies benefiting from this change; others include Amazon, Google, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI, Oracle, and xAI. The new rule allows these companies and their subsidiaries to obtain controlled advanced computing equipment without applying for individual licenses, provided they are approved as recipients and end-users. This regulation primarily governs the U.S. approval required for the export, re-export, or transfer of these chips, servers, and systems. Practically, this provides convenience for tech giants establishing or expanding data center infrastructure in the UAE. While how Apple will utilize this new authorization remains to be seen, data center infrastructure is its most apparent use case. This move represents the U.S. adjusting its export controls on critical AI hardware supply chains toward a more practical direction.",
